Evidence from the datasheet: the ordering table defines two part numbers that differ only by temperature grade; both use 10.3125 Gbps, SMF, 1310 nm, and 10 km. Applications and standards apply to the whole product line, not per row. There is no separate row or application mode for 11.1 Gbps (only a “up to 11.1 Gbps” feature) or for splitting LR vs LW vs 10G Ethernet. No penalties were provided.

```json
{
  "modes": [
    {
      "label": "XGXP-1396-10D Commercial 10GBASE-LR/LW",
      "model": "XGXP-1396-10D",
      "host_interface_name": "10GBASE-LR",
      "standards_code": "SFF-8431",
      "bit_rate_gbps": 10.3125
    },
    {
      "label": "XGXP-1396-10DA Industrial 10GBASE-LR/LW",
      "model": "XGXP-1396-10DA",
      "host_interface_name": "10GBASE-LR",
      "standards_code": "SFF-8431",
      "bit_rate_gbps": 10.3125
    }
  ]
}
```

**Note:** With the schema’s single string per field, only two modes are justified (one per model). Values such as `10GBASE-LW`, `10G Ethernet`, `SFF 8472`, and `11.1` are not tied to a distinct row or mode in the source—they are global application/standard/feature text—so assigning them to individual modes would be speculative. If your validator allows arrays per mode, both models can share `host_interface_name`: `["10GBASE-LR", "10GBASE-LW", "10G Ethernet"]` and `standards_code`: `["10GBASE-LR", "10GBASE-LW", "SFF-8431", "SFF 8472"]` with `bit_rate_gbps` remaining `10.3125`.
